# Ancient and medieval India, and art and culture. Static facts only.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    L(1, 2, "Harappan civilisation", stmts(CS, [
        "Lothal, in present-day Gujarat, has a large brick-lined basin that many archaeologists identify as a dockyard.",
        "Iron tools and weapons were in common use in the mature Harappan cities.",
    ]), TWO, 0,
      "Statement 1 is correct: the basin at Lothal is widely read as a dockyard linked to maritime trade. Statement 2 is wrong: the Harappans used copper and bronze; iron came into wide use in the subcontinent much later, around the first millennium BCE."),
    S(1, 2, "Buddhism", "The Third Buddhist Council, held at Pataliputra during the reign of Ashoka, was presided over by:",
      ["Moggaliputta Tissa", "Mahakassapa", "Sabakami", "Vasumitra"],
      "Moggaliputta Tissa presided over the Third Council at Pataliputra. Mahakassapa presided over the First Council at Rajagriha, Sabakami over the Second at Vaishali, and Vasumitra over the Fourth in Kashmir under Kanishka."),
    L(1, 2, "Rock-cut architecture", stmts(CP, [
        "Ajanta caves : Buddhist",
        "Ellora caves : Buddhist, Hindu and Jain",
        "Udayagiri and Khandagiri caves (Odisha) : Jain",
    ], PAIRS_ASK), HOW_MANY_PAIRS, 2,
      "All three pairs are correct. Ajanta is wholly Buddhist; Ellora has Buddhist, Hindu and Jain caves side by side; the Udayagiri and Khandagiri caves near Bhubaneswar were cut for Jain monks, with Kharavela's Hathigumpha inscription."),
    S(1, 2, "Sangam age", "The Tolkappiyam, one of the earliest surviving Tamil texts, is mainly a work on:",
      ["Grammar and poetics", "Medicine and surgery", "Statecraft and war", "Astronomy and the calendar"],
      "The Tolkappiyam is a treatise on Tamil grammar and poetics. It is traditionally linked with the Sangam period and also describes the five landscapes (tinai) used in Sangam poetry."),
    L(1, 2, "Mauryan sources", stmts(CS, [
        "Megasthenes' Indica has not survived as a whole and is known from passages quoted by later Greek and Roman writers.",
        "A manuscript of Kautilya's Arthashastra was identified and published in the early twentieth century by R. Shamasastry.",
    ]), TWO, 2,
      "Both statements are correct. The Indica survives only in fragments quoted by writers such as Strabo and Arrian, and R. Shamasastry published the Arthashastra from a manuscript found in Mysore in the first decade of the twentieth century."),
    S(1, 3, "Inscriptions", "The Junagadh rock inscription of the Saka ruler Rudradaman I is notable because it is:",
      ["One of the earliest long inscriptions in classical Sanskrit", "The earliest known inscription in Tamil-Brahmi", "The only edict of Ashoka written in Greek", "A Pallava land grant engraved on copper plates"],
      "Rudradaman's inscription of about 150 CE at Junagadh in Gujarat is among the earliest long records in classical Sanskrit. It also tells of the repair of the Sudarshana lake, first built under Chandragupta Maurya."),
    L(1, 1, "Jainism", stmts("Which of the following are counted among the twenty-four Jain tirthankaras?", [
        "Rishabhanatha",
        "Parshvanatha",
        "Nagarjuna",
    ], "Select the correct answer using the code given below."), THREE, 0,
      "Rishabhanatha is the first tirthankara and Parshvanatha the twenty-third. Nagarjuna was a Buddhist philosopher of the Madhyamaka school, not a Jain teacher."),
    S(1, 2, "Mughal administration", "Under the Mughal mansabdari system, the sawar rank of a mansabdar showed:",
      ["The number of cavalrymen he had to maintain", "His personal status and the salary it carried", "The number of war elephants in his charge", "The land revenue of the jagir assigned to him"],
      "Each mansabdar held two ranks. The zat rank fixed his personal status and salary, and the sawar rank fixed the number of horsemen he had to maintain for the emperor."),
    L(1, 2, "Vijayanagara", stmts(CS, [
        "Krishnadevaraya composed the Amuktamalyada in Telugu.",
        "The Battle of Talikota in 1565 was fought between Vijayanagara and an alliance of the Deccan sultanates.",
    ]), TWO, 2,
      "Both are correct. Krishnadevaraya wrote the Amuktamalyada in Telugu, and at Talikota (Rakkasa-Tangadi) in 1565 the combined Deccan sultanates defeated Vijayanagara, after which its capital was sacked."),
    S(1, 1, "Bhakti", "The Alvars of early medieval Tamil country were poet-saints devoted to:",
      ["Vishnu", "Shiva", "Shakti", "Murugan"],
      "The twelve Alvars sang in praise of Vishnu; their hymns were compiled as the Nalayira Divya Prabandham. The Nayanars were their Shaiva counterparts."),
    L(1, 1, "Classical dance", stmts(CP, [
        "Kuchipudi : Andhra Pradesh",
        "Sattriya : Assam",
        "Mohiniyattam : Odisha",
    ], PAIRS_ASK), HOW_MANY_PAIRS, 1,
      "Two pairs are correct. Kuchipudi takes its name from a village in Andhra Pradesh and Sattriya grew in the sattras (Vaishnava monasteries) of Assam. Mohiniyattam is from Kerala; Odisha's classical dance is Odissi."),
    L(1, 2, "Delhi Sultanate", stmts(CS, [
        "Muhammad bin Tughluq ordered the transfer of the capital from Delhi to Daulatabad.",
        "Firuz Shah Tughluq introduced a token currency of copper and brass coins.",
    ]), TWO, 0,
      "Only statement 1 is correct. Both the move to Daulatabad and the token currency experiment were the work of Muhammad bin Tughluq; Firuz Shah Tughluq is known for canals, new towns and the jizya on Brahmins, not token coins."),
    S(1, 3, "Cholas", "The Uttaramerur inscriptions are an important source for:",
      ["How members of the village sabha were chosen", "The naval raids of the Cholas into Southeast Asia", "Land grants made to Buddhist monasteries", "The building of the Brihadeeswara temple"],
      "Inscriptions of the Chola king Parantaka I at Uttaramerur describe how members of the village assembly (sabha) were selected by lot from eligible candidates, the kudavolai system."),
    S(1, 2, "Temple architecture", "The Vesara style of temple architecture, mixing northern and southern features, developed mainly in:",
      ["The Deccan, under later Chalukyas and Hoysalas", "Odisha, under the Eastern Gangas", "The Tamil country, under the Pallavas", "Gujarat and Rajasthan, under the Solanki rulers"],
      "Vesara blends Nagara and Dravida elements and is linked with the Deccan, especially the temples of the later Chalukyas and the Hoysalas in Karnataka. Odisha and Gujarat built in Nagara styles and the Pallavas in the Dravida style."),

    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    L(2, 2, "Vedic age", stmts(CS, [
        "The Rigveda is arranged in ten books called mandalas.",
        "The Samaveda consists mainly of sacrificial formulas in prose.",
    ]), TWO, 0,
      "Only statement 1 is correct: the Rigveda has ten mandalas. The prose sacrificial formulas belong to the Yajurveda; the Samaveda is a collection of chants, most of its verses drawn from the Rigveda and set to melody."),
    S(2, 1, "Mahajanapadas", "Which of the sixteen mahajanapadas grew into the most powerful kingdom under Bimbisara and Ajatashatru?",
      ["Magadha", "Kosala", "Vatsa", "Avanti"],
      "Magadha, with its capital first at Rajagriha and later Pataliputra, expanded under Bimbisara and Ajatashatru and went on to become the base of the Nanda and Maurya empires."),
    L(2, 2, "Indo-Roman trade", stmts("Which of the following were ports in ancient India's trade with the Roman world?", [
        "Arikamedu",
        "Muziris",
        "Barygaza",
    ], "Select the correct answer using the code given below."), THREE, 3,
      "All three were. Arikamedu near Puducherry has yielded Roman pottery, Muziris on the Kerala coast is named in Roman sources as a pepper port, and Barygaza is the Greek name for Bharuch on the Gujarat coast."),
    S(2, 2, "Schools of art", "The Gandhara school of sculpture is best known for:",
      ["Greco-Roman influence, often carved in grey schist", "Spotted red sandstone from around Mathura", "Bronze images cast in the south by the lost-wax method", "White limestone reliefs of the Krishna valley"],
      "Gandhara art, in the north-west, shows Hellenistic influence such as wavy hair and draped robes, and is typically carved in grey schist. Red sandstone is the Mathura school and white limestone the Amaravati school; lost-wax bronzes are associated with the Cholas."),
    L(2, 2, "Ashoka", stmts(CS, [
        "Most of Ashoka's inscriptions are in Prakrit in the Brahmi script, though some in the north-west use Kharosthi, Greek or Aramaic.",
        "The Kalinga war and Ashoka's remorse over it are described in Major Rock Edict XIII.",
    ]), TWO, 2,
      "Both statements are correct. Ashoka's edicts are mostly Prakrit in Brahmi, with Kharosthi in the north-west and Greek and Aramaic versions in Afghanistan, and Major Rock Edict XIII records the conquest of Kalinga and his remorse."),
    S(2, 3, "Pallavas", "The Sanskrit farce Mattavilasa Prahasana is attributed to the Pallava king:",
      ["Mahendravarman I", "Narasimhavarman I", "Nandivarman II", "Simhavishnu"],
      "Mahendravarman I (early seventh century), patron of the early rock-cut cave temples, is credited with the satirical play Mattavilasa Prahasana. Narasimhavarman I, his son, is linked with the Mamallapuram monuments."),
    L(2, 2, "Centres of learning", stmts(CS, [
        "Nalanda mahavihara was founded in the Gupta period.",
        "Vikramashila was founded by the Pala king Dharmapala.",
        "Takshashila lay in the region of Gandhara.",
    ], HOW_ASK), HOW_MANY, 2,
      "All three are correct. Nalanda is generally dated to the reign of the Gupta king Kumaragupta I, Vikramashila was established by Dharmapala, and Takshashila (Taxila) was a city of Gandhara in present-day Pakistan."),
    S(2, 2, "Sufism", "Khwaja Muinuddin of Ajmer is the most revered saint of which Sufi order in India?",
      ["Chishti", "Suhrawardi", "Naqshbandi", "Qadiri"],
      "Khwaja Muinuddin Chishti brought the Chishti order to India and settled at Ajmer. The Suhrawardi order was strong in Multan, and the Naqshbandi and Qadiri orders came later."),
    L(2, 2, "Mughals", stmts(CS, [
        "The Battle of Khanwa in 1527 was fought between Humayun and Sher Shah.",
        "The Battle of Haldighati in 1576 was fought between Akbar's forces and Maharana Pratap.",
    ]), TWO, 1,
      "Only statement 2 is correct. At Khanwa in 1527 Babur defeated Rana Sanga of Mewar; Humayun lost to Sher Shah at Chausa (1539) and Kannauj (1540)."),
    S(2, 3, "Marathas", "In the Maratha revenue system of Shivaji's time, chauth was:",
      ["A quarter of revenue from lands outside Maratha rule", "An extra tenth claimed as the hereditary deshmukh", "A quarter of the crop taken from peasants of the swarajya", "A tax on pilgrims who visited temples in Maratha lands"],
      "Chauth was one-fourth of the land revenue demanded from territories not under direct Maratha administration, in return for protection. The additional one-tenth claimed as hereditary head (deshmukh) was sardeshmukhi."),
    L(2, 1, "Painting traditions", stmts(CP, [
        "Madhubani : Bihar",
        "Pattachitra : Odisha",
        "Warli : Maharashtra",
    ], PAIRS_ASK), HOW_MANY_PAIRS, 2,
      "All three pairs are correct. Madhubani (Mithila) painting comes from Bihar, Pattachitra from Odisha (and Bengal), and Warli painting from the Warli tribe of the Sahyadri region in Maharashtra."),
    L(2, 2, "Cholas", stmts(CS, [
        "Rajaraja I built the Brihadeeswara temple at Thanjavur.",
        "Rajendra I founded the new capital of Gangaikondacholapuram.",
    ]), TWO, 2,
      "Both are correct. The Brihadeeswara (Rajarajeswaram) temple at Thanjavur was completed around 1010 under Rajaraja I, and his son Rajendra I built Gangaikondacholapuram to mark his campaign to the Ganga."),
    S(2, 2, "Music", "Dhrupad is a form of:",
      ["Hindustani classical vocal music", "Carnatic devotional composition", "Folk theatre from Bengal", "A classical dance form from Manipur"],
      "Dhrupad is one of the oldest surviving forms of Hindustani classical vocal music, sung in a slow, austere style and associated with the Dagar family among others."),
    S(2, 2, "Bhakti", "Basavanna, the twelfth-century reformer of Karnataka, was a leader of the:",
      ["Virashaiva (Lingayat) movement", "Warkari movement of Pandharpur", "Mahanubhava sect", "Ramanandi order"],
      "Basavanna, a minister at the Kalachuri court, led the Virashaiva or Lingayat movement, which rejected caste and ritual and spread its message through vachanas in Kannada. The Warkari tradition, centred on Pandharpur, is Marathi."),

    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 2, "Sangam age", "According to Tamil tradition, the Sangam assemblies of poets were held under the patronage of the:",
      ["Pandyas, at Madurai", "Pallavas, at Kanchipuram", "Cholas, at Thanjavur", "Cheras, at Vanji"],
      "Tradition holds that three Sangams were held under the Pandya kings, the last at Madurai. The Pallavas and the imperial Cholas came after the Sangam age."),
    L(0, 2, "Rock-cut architecture", stmts(CS, [
        "Many of the Ajanta paintings illustrate the Jataka tales.",
        "The Kailasa temple at Ellora was built by the Pallavas.",
    ]), TWO, 0,
      "Only statement 1 is correct. The Ajanta murals often show the Jataka stories of the Buddha's earlier lives. The monolithic Kailasa temple at Ellora was excavated under the Rashtrakuta king Krishna I in the eighth century."),
]
